[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Form Actions & Server-Side Data in SvelteKit"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"SvelteKit provides a robust mechanism for handling data mutations through Form Actions. Form actions allow you to securely execute server-side code when a standard HTML form is submitted, supporting progressive enhancement out of the box."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Creating Form Actions in Server Files"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"To handle form submissions, you define a +page.server.js (or .ts) file alongside your page. You export an object named actions containing your server-side handler functions."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"export const actions = {
  default: async ({ request }) => {
    const data = await request.formData();
    const email = data.get("email");

    // Perform database operations here
    console.log("Saving email:", email);

    return { success: true };
  }
};"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Consuming Actions in Svelte Components"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"In your +page.svelte file, you can create a standard form element. SvelteKit automatically wires up the form to post to the corresponding server action without requiring custom JavaScript event listeners."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"<script>
  export let form;
</script>

<form method="POST">
  <label>
    Email:
    <input name="email" type="email" required />
  </label>
  <button type="submit">Subscribe</button>
</form>

{#if form?.success}
  <p>Successfully subscribed!</p>
{/if}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Named Actions"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"A single page can handle multiple form submissions by using named actions. By appending the action name to the query string of the form action attribute, SvelteKit routes the request to the correct function."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"// +page.server.js
export const actions = {
  login: async ({ request }) => {
    // Handle login logic
  },
  register: async ({ request }) => {
    // Handle registration logic
  }
};"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"To trigger a named action from the component, use the action attribute with the desired route suffix."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"<form method="POST" action="?/register">
  <input name="username" type="text" />
  <button type="submit">Register</button>
</form>"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Benefits of SvelteKit Form Actions"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Progressive Enhancement: Forms work natively even if JavaScript is disabled in the client browser."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Data Validation: Easily return validation errors and form states back to the client via the 'form' prop."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Security: Built-in request parsing prevents common vulnerabilities when properly structured."}]
